raw:
{
  "diagnosis": {
    "primary_pattern": "Spleen-Kidney Yang Deficiency with Qi-Blood Deficiency and Blood Stasis",
    "secondary_patterns": [
      "Yin Deficiency Heat Flaring Upward (manifesting as hot palms and sweaty soles despite underlying cold constitution)",
      "Qi Not Securing Blood (leading to purpura and bleeding tendency)"
    ],
    "affected_organs": [
      "Spleen (impaired blood and Qi production, digestive weakness)",
      "Kidney (Yang exhaustion, loss of bone marrow governance, constitutional decline)",
      "Heart (insomnia, spirit disturbance due to blood deficiency)",
      "Liver (blood stasis indicated by dark sublingual veins)"
    ],
    "pathomechanism": "At 74 years old, the patient's Kidney Yang has naturally declined with age, undermining the Essence (foundational life force) that governs bone marrow function. The Spleen, which relies on Kidney Yang as its root and is responsible for transforming food into Qi and Blood, has become correspondingly weakened. Without sufficient Spleen Yang and Kidney Yang, the bone marrow cannot generate adequate blood cells and platelets — manifesting as aplastic anemia. Simultaneously, Qi deficiency has caused the Qi mechanism (responsible for holding blood in vessels) to fail, resulting in purpura and bleeding. The dark sublingual veins indicate blood stagnation, a consequence of poor circulation from Yang deficiency. The patient exhibits a paradoxical heat pattern (hot palms, sweaty soles) due to Yin deficiency; Yin normally anchors and cools the body, and when deficient, rootless heat flares upward while the lower body (below the ankle) remains cold — a classic sign of simultaneous Yang collapse at the root and Yin deficiency at the surface. The insomnia with many dreams reflects both blood deficiency (unable to nourish the Heart and calm the Spirit) and Yin deficiency heat (agitating the Mind).",
    "disease_cause": "Primary cause: Aging and constitutional Kidney Yang deficiency. At 74, the patient's original Qi (Yuan Qi) naturally declines; Kidney governs this reserve and its failure precipitates bone marrow aplasia. Contributing causes: chronic illness or stress may have further compromised Spleen function, accelerating the collapse of blood production. The present aplastic anemia represents the endpoint of long-standing Spleen-Kidney Yang exhaustion."
  },
  "constitution": {
    "type": "Yang-Deficient Constitution",
    "characteristics": "Cold limbs despite warm environment, pale and lusterless complexion, tendency to feel cold easily, preference for warm foods and beverages, loose or soft stools, low energy even after sleep, poor appetite, easy bruising. The paradoxical hot palms and sweaty soles represent yin deficiency heat (a secondary manifestation) overlaid on the fundamental yang exhaustion.",
    "tendencies": "This constitution is prone to cold-type diseases, chronic fatigue disorders, poor wound healing, recurrent infections, edema, joint aches in cold weather, and blood-related disorders including bleeding tendency and anemia. Without intervention, it tends toward further constitutional collapse with age.",
    "percentage_match": "Strong match (approximately 85% confidence) — the cold limbs, pale complexion, fatigue, weak pulse, and response pattern to cold are unmistakable markers. The secondary yin deficiency heat signs (hot palms) add complexity but do not contradict the primary yang-deficient diagnosis."
  },
  "analysis": {
    "summary": "Case 08 presents with acquired aplastic anemia, a serious hematologic disorder unresponsive to conventional treatment, set against a clear pattern of Spleen-Kidney Yang Deficiency. The clinical picture is remarkably coherent: a 74-year-old woman in natural constitutional decline (aging → Kidney Yang exhaustion) now manifesting bone marrow failure, bleeding disorder, and systemic collapse of Qi and Yang metabolism. The tongue — blue-pale with dark sublingual veins — is pathognomonic: the pale body indicates blood deficiency and Yang collapse, while the darkened sublingual veins (indicating venous pooling and stasis) reveal impaired circulation from Qi deficiency and Yang insufficiency. The pulse, described as \"slippery, fine and weak,\" confirms profound deficiency; fine (thready) indicates blood-yin deficiency, weak indicates overall Qi-Yang collapse, and slippery in this context (rather than suggesting the pregnancy or phlegm-damp it normally indicates) points to impaired marrow function and fluid pathology characteristic of aplastic anemia.\n\nThe symptomatology aligns perfectly with this diagnosis. Marked fatigue and appetite loss reflect Spleen Qi and Yang deficiency, the metabolic engine that normally transforms food into usable Qi and Blood. Severe constipation (reported as a treatment side effect but also consistent with Spleen Yang weakness preventing proper bowel peristalsis) further depletes fluids. Purpura — spontaneous bruising and bleeding — reveals that Qi is failing in its classical role of \"securing\" blood within vessels (Qi Bu Tong Xue). The cold sensation below the ankles is a hallmark of Yang deficiency: Kidney Yang, which warms the lower body and the extremities, is exhausted. The insomnia with many dreams indicates Heart-Spirit (Shen) disturbance due to blood deficiency (blood nourishes the Heart), compounded by yin deficiency heat that agitates the Mind at night. The hot palms and sweaty soles appear contradictory but are understood in TCM as \"true cold with false heat\" (真寒假热) — the constitutional cold is so severe that it generates a compensatory, rootless heat that manifests in the extremities and on exertion, like a dying fire's last flare. The pale complexion and moon-shaped face (characteristic of chronic kidney disease and long-standing deficiency) underscore that this is not an acute illness but a constitutional exhaustion of decades, now unraveling.\n\nThe patient's age (74) and presentation (bone marrow failure, pale tongue, weak pulse, profound fatigue) confirm that this is primarily a Spleen-Kidney Yang Deficiency disorder, with secondary Yin Deficiency Heat and Blood Stasis as complications. The conventional diagnosis of aplastic anemia is the biomedical name; the TCM diagnosis is the underlying constitutional and metabolic pattern that has allowed the disease to develop. TCM treatment cannot replace conventional hematology (blood transfusions, immunosuppressants if indicated, monitoring) but can address the root deficiency and may support platelet and hemoglobin recovery by strengthening the Spleen's blood-generating capacity and the Kidney's governance of bone marrow. The prognosis depends heavily on whether any residual bone marrow function exists and whether the patient's constitutional Yang can be cautiously tonified without aggravating the secondary yin-heat.",
    "key_findings": {
      "from_inquiry": "Acquired aplastic anemia unresponsive to conventional care (indicating deep constitutional failure, not acute infection or toxin); marked fatigue (Qi deficiency); appetite loss (Spleen Yang weakness); severe constipation (Spleen Yang impairment); purpura (Qi not securing Blood); cold sensation below ankles (Kidney Yang deficiency); hot palms and sweaty soles (yin deficiency heat overlay); insomnia with many dreams (blood deficiency + yin heat); pale complexion and moon-shaped face (chronic deficiency and kidney involvement). The side effects (appetite loss, constipation) are not incidental — they reflect the patient's underlying weak constitution unable to tolerate or benefit from aggressive treatments.",
      "from_visual": "Tongue pale and blue-tinged (indicates Yang deficiency and blood deficiency; blue-green coloration can suggest stagnation and coldness); dark sublingual veins (classic sign of blood stasis and impaired venous return, consistent with Qi deficiency and poor Yang circulation); pale complexion (blood deficiency, Qi collapse); moon-shaped face (indicates chronic kidney disease and constitutional exhaustion, typical of long-standing Kidney deficiency in the elderly). These visual signs together paint a picture of systemic Yang-Qi collapse with secondary blood stasis.",
      "from_pulse": "Thready (fine) pulse indicates blood and yin deficiency, inability to fill the pulse. Weak pulse indicates overall Qi and Yang collapse, lack of driving force. Slippery pulse in context of aplastic anemia likely reflects impaired hematopoietic function and fluid pathology (bone marrow unable to produce cells, leading to altered blood viscosity or plasma composition). The combination \"slippery, fine, and weak\" is unusual; in isolation, slippery might suggest phlegm-damp or pregnancy, but here it reflects the pathophysiology of marrow failure — impaired cell production yet excessive fluid. All three qualities together confirm profound constitutional deficiency.",
      "from_other": "Age 74 is critical: natural Essence and Kidney Yang exhaustion with aging. The failure of conventional treatment (hematinic agents, possible immunosuppressants) suggests the disorder is constitutional, not responsive to symptomatic supplementation — the Spleen-Kidney foundation is too weak to rebuild blood production without addressing the root Yang deficiency. The chronicity (this is not acute onset but a long-standing constitution finally unraveling) supports a Yang Deficiency Constitution diagnosis rather than, for example, a Heat-Toxin pattern that would present acutely."
    },
    "pattern_rationale": "The diagnosis of Spleen-Kidney Yang Deficiency with Qi-Blood Deficiency and Blood Stasis is supported by convergent evidence from all Four Examinations:\n\n**Inquiry (Problem History):** The patient is 74 years old, an age at which Kidney Yang naturally declines per TCM (decline begins at age 56 in women per Classical texts, accelerating by 70s). Aplastic anemia in the elderly is often constitutional, reflecting exhaustion of marrow-generating capacity. The symptom triad of fatigue + appetite loss + constipation is pathognomonic for Spleen Yang deficiency (Spleen governs transformation and transportation of food; without Yang, digestion fails). Cold extremities below the ankles point to Kidney Yang deficiency (Kidney Yang warms the lower body; its loss leaves extremities cold). Purpura indicates Qi deficiency has impaired the Qi mechanism of \"securing\" blood within vessels — a classical TCM concept and a direct consequence of systemic Qi collapse. Insomnia with dreams indicates Heart-Spirit (Shen) disturbance, which in TCM has three primary causes: (a) blood deficiency (blood is the material basis of the Spirit), (b) Spleen deficiency (Spleen generates Blood), and (c) Yin deficiency heat (agitates the Spirit at night). All three are present here. The hot palms and sweaty soles, seemingly contradictory, are explained as yin deficiency heat — in deep Yang deficiency (\"true cold\"), the yin fluids become depleted and exhausted, generating a rootless compensatory heat that flares in the extremities (the most yang areas) and on exertion. This is \"true cold with false heat\" (真寒假热) and is resolved not by cold herbs but by warming and tonifying Yang (which restores the Yin fluid through metabolic restoration), a key clinical insight.\n\n**Visual Examination (Tongue & Face):** The pale and blue-tinged tongue directly indicates Yang deficiency (cold, loss of normal warmth and color) and blood deficiency (paleness indicates insufficient blood to color the tongue tissue). The blue-green tinge (slight cyanosis or stagnation) indicates cold and poor circulation. Dark, distended sublingual veins are a key sign of blood stasis, impaired venous return, and Qi deficiency — Qi normally drives circulation, and without it, blood pools and darkens. The pale complexion confirms blood deficiency and Yang collapse — the face loses its normal pink rosy tone when both blood and Yang are deficient. The moon-shaped face (round, pale, slightly puffy) is characteristic of chronic kidney deficiency and nutritional depletion; in TCM, it reflects Kidney Yang deficiency and loss of the Kidney's ability to govern fluid metabolism (leading to retention and puffiness). Together, these visual signs unmistakably point to Yang and Blood deficiency.\n\n**Palpation (Pulse):** The thready (fine) pulse quality indicates insufficient substance to fill the vessel; this is the classic pulse of blood deficiency or yin deficiency. The weak pulse indicates insufficient driving force; Qi normally propels the pulse, and when Qi is deficient, the pulse becomes weak and forceless. The slippery quality is more complex in this context: classically, slippery suggests phlegm-damp or pregnancy, but in the setting of bone marrow failure, it reflects impaired cell production and altered blood composition — the blood is actually thinner and more fluid because the marrow is not producing adequate red blood cells and platelets. The combination \"fine, weak, and slippery\" is thus consistent with severe bone marrow failure: thready from blood deficiency, weak from Qi deficiency, slippery from altered hematologic composition. No other common pattern (e.g., Liver Heat, Stomach Fire, Phlegm-Damp) would produce this pulse pattern in the context of aplastic anemia.\n\n**Pathophysiological Coherence:** The diagnosis explains the hematologic disorder: in TCM, the Spleen and Kidney together govern blood production and bone marrow function (Kidney is said to govern the bone marrow — 肾主骨髓). Spleen transforms food into Qi and Blood; Kidney Yang is the foundational fire beneath this transformation. At age 74, Kidney Yang is naturally depleted, and the Spleen, depending on Kidney Yang as its functional root, has lost the ability to generate blood. Furthermore, Qi deficiency impairs the \"securing\" mechanism, leading to bleeding. Blood stasis (dark sublingual veins) results from Qi deficiency (Qi moves blood) and Yang deficiency (warmth drives circulation). The insomnia results from blood deficiency (cannot nourish Heart) and yin heat (compensatory heat from deep Yang deficiency agitates the Spirit). The hot palms and sweaty soles, far from contradicting the diagnosis, are explained as the surface manifestation of yin deficiency heat — the deepest cold generates the most stubborn superficial heat, a classical TCM paradox. The constipation reflects Spleen Yang weakness and the secondary yin deficiency (dry stools from yin deficiency, weak peristalsis from Yang deficiency). The appetite loss reflects Spleen Yang inability to process food. Every symptom, every finding locks into this pattern like a key; no other pattern fits as completely.\n\n**Exclusion of Alternative Patterns:** Could this be primarily a Blood Stasis pattern? No — blood stasis alone would not produce such profound fatigue, appetite loss, cold limbs, and weak pulse. Could it be Liver Heat? No — Liver Heat would present with irritability, red eyes, bitter taste, and rapid pulse; this patient is pale and weak. Could it be Yin Deficiency? Partially — yin deficiency is present (hot palms, insomnia), but yin alone cannot explain the cold limbs, constipation, and appetite loss; yin deficiency typically presents with heat signs throughout, not localized cold. The presence of cold below the ankles and pale complexion definitively indicates Yang deficiency as the primary pattern. Could it be Spleen Qi Deficiency alone? Partially — Spleen Qi deficiency would explain fatigue, appetite loss, and loose stool, but would not explain the cold limbs, the yin deficiency heat (hot palms), or the degree of blood deficiency. The diagnosis of Spleen-Kidney Yang Deficiency with Qi-Blood Deficiency and Blood Stasis is the most coherent, parsimonious, and complete explanation of all clinical findings."
  },
  "treatment": {
    "therapeutic_principle": "Warm and tonify Spleen-Kidney Yang to restore the constitutional foundation; tonify Qi and nourish Blood to restore the material basis of life; move and invigorate Blood to resolve stasis and restore circulation; cautiously nourish Yin to modulate the secondary heat without further chilling the Yang. The treatment approach is fundamentally restorative and constitutional, not symptomatic or suppressant.",
    "treatment_method": "Warm Yang and fortify Spleen (温阳健脾); tonify Qi and supplement Blood (补气养血); invigorate Blood and transform stasis (活血化瘀); carefully supplement Yin to control the rootless heat without impairing Yang (小心滋阴以制虚热而不伤阳). Treatment will be gradual and gentle, avoiding aggressive tonification that might overwhelm the weak digestive capacity, and will emphasize dietary therapy and lifestyle as the foundation, with herbal medicine as the augmentation."
  },
  "recommendations": {
    "food_therapy": {
      "beneficial": [
        "Red dates (jujubes) — sweet and warm, tonify Qi and nourish Blood; specifically strengthen Spleen function and provide natural sugars that support bone marrow recovery; eat 3-5 daily, preferably stewed in warm water or congee",
        "Chicken (particularly free-range or locally-raised) — warm and tonifying to Qi; supplies bioavailable protein to support blood and marrow production; less heating than beef, more easily digested; use in soups with warming spices like ginger",
        "Goji berries (gou qi zi) — sweet and neutral-warm, tonify both Kidney Yin and Yang and nourish Essence (Jing), the root of bone marrow function; add 1 tablespoon to soups or morning congee daily",
        "Black sesame seeds — sweet and neutral-warm, tonify blood and Yin while warming Yang; high in iron (bioavailable form) and minerals; grind into a paste and add to warm sesame milk or congee, 1-2 tablespoons daily",
        "Millet congee (milled grain cooked into a soft porridge) — warm, tonify Qi and nourish the Spleen; millet is the most easily digestible grain for weak digestive systems; make with warming additions (ginger slices, red dates, a pinch of cinnamon) and eat warm for breakfast"
      ],
      "recipes": [
        "Spleen-Kidney Warming Congee: Cook 1 cup millet with 6 cups bone broth (chicken or vegetable); add 1 thumb of fresh ginger (sliced), 8-10 red dates (pitted), 1 tablespoon goji berries, and a pinch of ground cinnamon. Simmer on low heat for 1 hour until creamy. Season with a tiny amount of sea salt (salt nourishes Kidney in small amounts). Eat warm each morning. Purpose: Tonify Spleen-Kidney Yang, warm the digestive fire, nourish Blood, and provide easily-absorbed Qi and nutrients.",
        "Black Chicken (or regular chicken) and Astragalus Soup: Use 1 pound chicken (cut into chunks), 15g dried astragalus root (黄芪, huang qi), 10 red dates (pitted), 5 slices ginger, 1 liter water. Bring to boil, then simmer on low heat for 1.5 hours. The astragalus strongly tonifies Qi and supports marrow function; the chicken warms and supplements essence. Add a small pinch of salt at the end. Drink 1-2 bowls daily, 3-4 times per week. Purpose: Tonify Qi, warm Kidney Yang, support blood production, and strengthen constitutional Qi to enhance the patient's own healing capacity."
      ]
    },
    "foods_to_avoid": [
      "Cold and raw foods — watermelon, cucumber, bitter melon, uncooked salads, cold drinks, ice water; these damage the Spleen Yang directly and impair digestion, worsening Qi deficiency and appetite loss; warming the body is essential to recovery",
      "Greasy and difficult-to-digest foods — fried foods, rich creams, excessive oils, pork fat; these overwhelm the weak Spleen-Yang digestive fire, leading to stagnation and poor nutrient absorption; choose lighter proteins like chicken, fish, and eggs",
      "Refined sugars, white flour, and processed foods — these provide empty calories and deplete Qi (sugar especially drains Spleen function); they do not nourish the marrow and should be replaced with whole grains, natural sweeteners (honey, dates), and whole foods"
    ],
    "lifestyle": [
      "Prioritize warmth: Keep the abdomen, lower back, and feet warm at all times. Wear layers, particularly over the Kidney region (lower back). Cold damages the already-exhausted Kidney Yang. A heating pad on the lower abdomen for 15-20 minutes daily (over clothing) can support Kidney Yang tonification.",
      "Gentle movement aligned with capacity: Tai Chi (especially seated or very slow standing forms), Qigong breathing exercises (Ba Duan Jin, Six Sounds), or slow walking in sunshine are ideal. These warm the body, promote circulation without exhausting Qi, and support the Kidney-Spleen foundation. Avoid strenuous exercise, which depletes Qi in someone this deficient. 10-15 minutes daily is sufficient.",
      "Sleep and rest discipline: Aim to be in bed by 22:00, asleep by 23:00, and awake by 06:00 or 07:00. The Kidney governs the night and the root of sleep; gentle adherence to early sleep supports Kidney Yang recovery. If insomnia persists, do not fight it — instead, practice calm breathing and visualization (imagining warmth at the Kidney area) rather than becoming frustrated.",
      "Emotional calm and reduced worry: Worry depletes Spleen Qi specifically (each emotion has an organ). Practice mindfulness or gentle meditation focused on the lower abdomen (Dantian) for 5-10 minutes daily. Avoid stressful situations and media; cultivate gratitude and gentleness toward the body's recovery. Encourage the patient to accept that healing is slow and that rest is active medicine in this condition."
    ],
    "acupoints": [
      "Guanyuan (CV4, 关元) — Located on the midline of the abdomen, 3 cun (approximately 3 finger-widths) below the umbilicus (navel). Gentle circular massage with warm palm for 2-3 minutes daily, preferably in the morning while warm. This is the Gate of Original Qi (Yuan Qi) and powerfully tonifies Kidney Yang, the root of all constitutional recovery; it is the most important point for this patient.",
      "Zusanli (ST36, 足三里) — Located on the anterior (front) leg, 3 cun below the inferior border of the kneecap (below the bony prominence at the knee), one finger-breadth lateral (outward) from the anterior tibial crest (the sharp bone running down the front of the shin). Massage with circular motions using the thumb or knuckle for 2-3 minutes daily, preferably in the morning or afternoon. This point powerfully tonifies Qi and supports Spleen function; it is historically considered a primary longevity point and is essential for blood cell production.",
      "Shenshu (UB23, 肾俞) — Located on the back, on the Bladder meridian, at the level of the lower border of the spinous process of the 2nd lumbar vertebra (approximately at the level of the waist, 1.5 cun lateral to the midline spine). Have the patient lie prone (face-down) and gently massage the area with warm palms in circular motions for 2-3 minutes daily. This is the Back-Shu point of the Kidney and directly tonifies Kidney Yang and essence; warmth applied here is especially beneficial.",
      "Shenmen (HT7, 神门) — Located on the wrist, in the crease of the inner wrist (on the ulnar/pinky side), at the medial end of the wrist crease. Apply gentle pressure with the thumb and massage in small circles for 1-2 minutes, 1-2 times daily (especially before bed). This calms the Spirit (Shen) and nourishes the Heart; it is essential for treating the insomnia and heart disturbance from blood deficiency."
    ],
    "exercise": [
      "Tai Chi (simplified 8-movement form or seated variation): 10-15 minutes daily, preferably in the morning or early afternoon in sunlight. Tai Chi warms the body, promotes circulation, and tonifies Qi without excessive exertion. For a 74-year-old with severe fatigue, seated Tai Chi hand movements or a very slow standing form (holding onto a chair for balance) is appropriate. The gentle, flowing nature supports Yang circulation and calm.",
      "Qigong breathing — Ba Duan Jin (Eight Pieces of Brocade) or Liuzijue (Six Healing Sounds): 5-10 minutes daily, focusing on the movements associated with Kidney and Spleen. Specifically: the Kidney sound (\"chui\" / \"chuuuu\") exhaled while gently massaging the lower abdomen and lower back, and the Spleen sound (\"ho\" / \"hooo\") exhaled while gently massaging the upper abdomen. These directly support organ tonification and circulate Qi."
    ],
    "emotional_care": "The patient is facing a serious, life-threatening illness at an advanced age. Emotionally, she may feel despair about the failure of conventional treatment and anxiety about her mortality. In TCM philosophy, the Spleen governs the Yi (intellect and thought), and when Spleen Qi is deficient, the mind becomes scattered, anxious, and prone to rumination. Encourage the patient to: (1) Accept that healing is slow and that small improvements (sleeping better, fewer bruises, slightly more energy) are victories; (2) Shift from seeking a cure to supporting recovery — the language matters; (3) Spend time with loved ones and engage in gentle, joyful activities (listening to music, reading, gentle nature viewing); (4) Practice gratitude for what the body still does, rather than focusing on its failures; (5) Consider that in TCM, the Kidney houses the Will (Zhi, 志), the deep determination to live; reconnecting with purpose and will often correlates with improved constitutional recovery. Gentle guided imagery focused on warmth and light descending into the lower abdomen and bone marrow can be psychologically and physiologically supportive.",
    "herbal_formulas": [
      {
        "name": "Four Gentlemen Decoction with Warming Modifications (四君子汤加温阳之品)",
        "ingredients": [
          "Ginseng (Ren Shen, 人参) — 9g — tonifies Qi strongly",
          "White Atractylodes (Bai Zhu, 白术) — 9g — tonifies Spleen Qi and transforms dampness",
          "Poria (Fu Ling, 茯苓) — 9g — fortifies Spleen and quiets the Heart",
          "Prepared Rehmannia (Shu Di Huang, 熟地黄) — 9g — nourishes Blood and Yin",
          "Cinnamon bark (Rou Gui, 肉桂) — 3g (only 3g to warm without excessive heating) — warms Kidney Yang and drives circulation",
          "Dried ginger (Gan Jiang, 干姜) — 3g — warms the Spleen and aids digestion",
          "Donkey-hide gelatin (E Jiao, 阿胶, added at end, do not boil directly) — 9g — strongly nourishes Blood and stops bleeding (critical for purpura)",
          "Red dates (Da Zao, 大枣) — 4 pieces (pitted) — tonifies Qi and nourishes Blood",
          "Licorice (Gan Cao, 甘草) — 3g — harmonizes the formula and tonifies Qi"
        ],
        "dosage": "Decoct (simmer gently for 40-50 minutes) in 3 cups water down to 1.5 cups. Strain. Add E Jiao (donkey-hide gelatin) to the warm strained decoction and stir until dissolved (do not boil the gelatin directly as heat destroys its efficacy). Divide into 2 portions. Take one portion in the morning (warm, 30 minutes before food) and one in the evening (warm, before bed). Take once daily for 14 days, then rest 3 days before repeating. Continue for a minimum of 8-12 weeks (ideally until blood counts show recovery).",
        "purpose": "This is a primary constitutional tonification formula designed specifically for Spleen-Kidney Yang deficiency with blood deficiency. The base (Four Gentlemen) tonifies Qi and nourishes Blood; the warming additions (cinnamon, ginger) restore Kidney Yang and Spleen digestive function; the E Jiao specifically addresses the bleeding tendency (purpura) and strengthens blood-nourishing capacity; the combination directly addresses bone marrow failure by supporting both the foundational Yang and the blood-generating capacity of the Spleen. This formula is gentle enough for long-term use and should form the backbone of herbal therapy."
      },
      {
        "name": "Supplementing the Middle and Augmenting the Qi Decoction (补中益气汤)",
        "ingredients": [
          "Astragalus (Huang Qi, 黄芪) — 15g — tonifies Qi strongly and supports immune function; essential for marrow recovery",
          "Ginseng (Ren Shen, 人参) — 9g (or substitute Codonopsis/党参 if ginseng is cost-prohibitive) — primary Qi tonic",
          "White Atractylodes (Bai Zhu, 白术) — 6g — fortifies Spleen",
          "Prepared Rehmannia (Shu Di Huang, 熟地黄) — 9g — nourishes Blood",
          "Bupleurum (Chai Hu, 柴胡) — 3g — moves Qi gently, prevents stagnation",
          "Tangerine peel (Chen Pi, 陈皮) — 3g — aids digestion and Qi movement",
          "Licorice (Gan Cao, 甘草) — 3g — harmonizes",
          "Red dates (Da Zao, 大枣) — 3 pieces (pitted) — tonifies Qi and Blood",
          "Ginger (fresh, Sheng Jiang, 生姜) — 3-4 slices — warms and aids digestion"
        ],
        "dosage": "Decoct for 40-50 minutes in 3 cups water down to 1.5 cups. Strain. Divide into 2 portions and take warm, one in the morning and one in the evening, 30 minutes before meals, for 14 days. Rest 3 days. Repeat for 8-12 weeks minimum. This formula can be alternated with the Four Gentlemen formula above (e.g., take Four Gentlemen for 2 weeks, then switch to this formula for 2 weeks, then rotate back) to prevent qi stagnation and maintain diverse support.",
        "purpose": "This classical formula is particularly valuable for Qi deficiency with blood deficiency affecting the bone marrow. The astragalus is exceptional for tonifying the Wei-Qi (defensive Qi) and supporting immune function — critical because aplastic anemia leaves the patient vulnerable to infection. This formula can be used as an adjunct or rotation partner with the Four Gentlemen formula to provide variation and prevent adaptation. The bupleurum prevents stagnation of Qi (a risk with pure tonification formulas in deficient patients with weak digestive fire)."
      }
    ]
  },
  "precautions": {
    "warning_signs": [
      "Major hemorrhage — bleeding from gums, blood in urine, blood in stools, vomiting blood, or nosebleeds that do not stop after 10-15 minutes of direct pressure — seek emergency medical care immediately; do not delay for TCM treatment",
      "Signs of serious infection — fever above 38.5°C (101.3°F), severe chills, confusion, difficulty breathing, or severe headache — due to very low white blood cells, infection can progress rapidly; seek emergency care at once",
      "Severe dizziness or fainting — may indicate dangerously low hemoglobin; check pulse and lie down, but seek medical evaluation same day",
      "Chest pain or severe shortness of breath — may indicate cardiac stress from severe anemia; seek emergency care",
      "Failure to improve after 8-12 weeks of TCM treatment combined with ongoing conventional monitoring — if blood counts do not show any rise and the patient does not report improved energy, appetite, or sleep despite adherence to treatment, escalate for re-evaluation"
    ],
    "contraindications": [
      "Do NOT attempt to use TCM herbs alone as the sole treatment for aplastic anemia — this is a life-threatening condition requiring ongoing conventional hematologic monitoring and possible transfusions or immunosuppressive therapy; TCM is complementary, supportive, and constitutional, not curative of marrow failure on its own",
      "Do NOT use stimulating or heating herbs (such as high-dose ginseng, strong warming tonics, or herbs that move blood aggressively) without professional supervision — in someone this Yang-deficient, overstimulation can precipitate arrhythmia, bleeding, or other crises; tonification must be gentle and gradual",
      "Do NOT recommend strenuous exercise, hot yoga, saunas, or rapid detoxification protocols — these exhaust Qi in someone already profoundly deficient and can worsen hemorrhagic tendency; warmth is needed, but gentle warmth, not aggressive heat",
      "Do NOT recommend iron supplementation without hematologist coordination — although blood deficiency is present, the underlying bone marrow failure means the body cannot effectively use iron; iron supplements may accumulate and cause toxicity; any iron therapy must be coordinated with CBC monitoring"
    ],
    "special_notes": "This patient requires close coordination between TCM and conventional medicine. Her hematologist should continue to monitor CBC (complete blood count, including platelet and hemoglobin levels) every 2-4 weeks. Any sudden drop in counts or sign of bleeding requires immediate medical evaluation. TCM treatment aims to support marrow recovery by strengthening the Spleen-Kidney constitutional foundation; improvement in blood counts will be slow (weeks to months). Psychological support is critical: at 74 with a serious diagnosis, the patient may experience despair. Emphasize that TCM treatment is a commitment to slow, gentle restoration, not a promise of cure. Small improvements in energy, appetite, sleep quality, or reduced bruising are meaningful victories and should be celebrated. If the patient requires blood transfusions, these should not be withheld due to TCM treatment — transfusions are lifesaving and compatible with TCM support. Finally, aplastic anemia in the elderly has a variable prognosis; some patients improve with treatment, others stabilize, and some continue to decline. TCM cannot change the inherent bone marrow biology, but it can optimize constitutional recovery, reduce comorbidity, and improve quality of life while the medical team manages the hematologic crisis."
  },
  "follow_up": {
    "timeline": "Reassess every 3-4 weeks initially. At each reassessment: (1) Ask about energy level, appetite, bowel regularity, sleep quality, and frequency/severity of bruising; (2) Check blood work (CBC) with the hematologist's office to track hemoglobin, platelets, and white blood cells; (3) Assess pulse and tongue for any shifts in the pattern; (4) Adjust herbal formulas if necessary based on response. If the patient shows improvement in subjective symptoms and/or blood counts, continue the treatment protocol. If no improvement after 8-12 weeks, consider whether the patient has adequate digestive capacity to absorb herbal medicine, whether herbal compliance is actually occurring, and whether the diagnosis may need refinement (possible secondary pathology).",
    "expected_improvement": "Over the first 4-6 weeks, expect subjective improvements before objective (CBC) changes: the patient may report better sleep, improved appetite, slightly more energy, or fewer new bruises. By 8-12 weeks, if the bone marrow has any residual function, hemoglobin and platelet counts may begin to rise slightly. Full recovery (normalization of blood counts) is not guaranteed and may take 3-6 months or longer, depending on the reversibility of the marrow aplasia. Realistic goals are: (1) halt the decline in blood counts; (2) improve subjective well-being and quality of life; (3) reduce the frequency of transfusions if the patient is receiving them; (4) reduce infection risk by supporting constitutional Qi. The condition is serious, and the patient should understand that the goal is supportive recovery, not miraculous reversal.",
